Z||000033||frontal lobe||08-16-2001||10:50 AM||||EdsB52, I agree that things could still be a little touchy, so it is good for a while yet to be EXTRA careful to point out that we aren't attacking or fighting, just presenting viewpoints.  So that's the spirit of my reply, too.<P>I am not answering all the points for sake of keeping posts not too long, but here are just a couple.<P>I probably am now in the 2% of the wealthy (don't know-5%?, doesn't matter), but you mentioned lucky guy.  But it wasn't luck.  I started out lower-middle socioeconomic class.  Through hard work, saving, delayed gratification, and LONG years, I have been able to become "wealthy".  I did get assistance from "the government", but they really got that money from individual taxpayers like yourself.  So thanks to YOU GUYS for loaning me the money for school.  However, I DID PAY IT BACK WITH INTEREST, SO IT DIDN'T ULTIMATELY COST YOU.  <P>That is my main question with how much money is finally enough to be my fair share?  Because there is some ultimately fixed cost for the services we use, the money we pay above that is redistributed to other americans.  This is a sincere question:  are you really saying that yes, you want other american's income to be GIVEN to you for work you haven't done?  Because that is what is happening all the time.<P>Finally, I was fascinated by your desire for freedom from speech comment.  Apparently specifically regarding religion.  So you are FOR freedom of speech, unless it's speech you don't want to hear, and then you want the right to restrict THAT speech.  Wow.  That really honestly portrays the general, honest thoughts of liberals.  And yet, as a conservative (generally-the bipartisan comment was true), although I don't like having to hear certain speech I'm unwilling to restrict their freedom for the sake of mine.<P>Sorry, already too long.  Reiteration--not mad or attacking, just discussing.||||reg||1
Z||000034||Cheever3000||08-16-2001||10:54 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<BR>$$$$$ for bible schools<HR></BLOCKQUOTE>I know I'm talking to a brick wall here, but ray: private Christian schools <B>don't <I>want</I></B> government money, because there are strings attached.  Along with gov't money comes gov't control.  All of the private Christian schools with whom I've had first-hand relations are making absolutely certain they don't take <I>anything</I> from the federal government.  They make a lot of sacrifices to do that, and it's why their teachers make so much less than in public schools.  The tuition they charge goes a long way (public schools should learn from them how to reduce waste and cut costs).<P>For your information, they don't exist because someone says "I think I'll start a school and push my religion on everybody".  They exist because grassroots Americans <B><I>support</I></B> them.  Not just by sending their kids there, but by volunteering their own time, sweat, talents and abilities to help out. <P>Aw, there's no reason to go on, I know you won't get it.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000035||ray b||08-16-2001||11:35 AM||||most of local bible based schools<BR>were created by fear of blacks in 60's<BR>as a responce to intergration of public schools.i hope they refuse the $$$ but don't think it will happen.but does that mean all schools will get $$$ or only the approved ones on W's list of paying bribers.<BR>some of the faiths i like to see get $$$$<BR>rastiafarians<BR>rainbow children<BR>native american church<BR>wickica<BR>muratisum<BR>zorasterisum<BR>zen<BR>shintoisum<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||5
Z||000036||frontal lobe||08-16-2001||11:47 AM||||rayb, if there is a school choice program where the parents get the money "from the government", and then the parents choose where the children go, I don't care if the parents decide to send them to a school such as you listed.  That is NOT government supporting certain schools.  The PARENTS have the FREEDOM to choose.<P>And I love this, "We can't take government money to give to support private schools".  The government got the money from private citizens.  So why it can't go back to private citizens for THEM to decide how to spend it is just beyond me.<P>And rayb, your contention that private schools were a reaction to desegration in the '60's is just ridiculous--at least in the north of the U.S. where I have lived my whole life.  I don't know about the south-MAYBE it's different, but I doubt it.<P>And again, rayb, I ENJOY your posts, so please, no offense at all is intended.||||reg||1
Z||000037||DRH||08-16-2001||12:19 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>originally posted by frontal lobe:<BR><B><BR>...<BR>And rayb, your contention that private schools were a reaction to desegration in the '60's is just ridiculous--at least in the north of the U.S. where I have lived my whole life.  I don't know about the south-MAYBE it's different, but I doubt it.<BR>...<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>rayb has a valid point here at least about SOME private schools. I grew up in Alabama. My school district, as well as a lot of others in the state, combined the white and "colored" schools the beginning of my 6th grade year in 1970. SEVERAL private schools, some religious based and some not, were established that same year. <P>It's not true in ALL cases though. Some private schools were already in existance. Many of those started accepting black students about that time (some actually did BEFORE the public schools did).<P>But, the MAJORITY of private schools around where I grew up were established in 1970 (give or take a year or two).<P><p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 08-16-2001).]||||reg||1
