Z||000040||EdsB52||08-16-2001||02:28 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by frontal lobe:<BR>[B][/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>We're cool F.L., we don't have to walk on eggshells and I don't take your opinion statements out of context. Feel free to post long. If people don't like it, let em scroll  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">.<P>"I probably am now in the 2% of the wealthy (don't know-5%?, doesn't matter), but you mentioned lucky guy."<P>Well, you're the second self-made millionaire that has disclosed that to me in this forum. I don't know you, so it wouldn't be fair to say you're lying, it just seems improbable to find millionaires driving cars that are worth under $5,000.<P>"I did get assistance from "the government", but they really got that money from individual taxpayers like yourself."<P>That’s great that one little push got you flying above the curve, but some people need to get kicked hard and never perform well, does that mean we cut off the assistance? If so, where is the threshold where we give up? If we refuse to help because someone isn't responding, is it an indication of that subject’s inaptitude or a statement of our (US citizens) uncompassionate nature?<P>"That is my main question with how much money is finally enough to be my fair share? " "This is a sincere question:  are you really saying that yes, you want other american's income to be GIVEN to you for work you haven't done?"<P>How much? That's a dynamic question that probably can best be answered with two words, flat tax. That's just an abbreviated answer; the real answer would take data and thousands of hours to figure out. If we had a flat tax there would be no misunderstanding what we owe and how fair is fair. Most rich people don't want this because they have accountants and lawyers finding ways to relieve them from paying anything. Redistribution of wealth isn't compensation for work done, it's relief to people that need it. Yes, I want Americas wealth redistributed to other Americans who are in need.<P>"So you are FOR freedom of speech, unless it's speech you don't want to hear, and then you want the right to restrict THAT speech.  Wow.  That really honestly portrays the general, honest thoughts of liberals."<P>This is how I replied to what you are referring:<P>"-Are you carefully saying the liberals don't revere religious speech as worthy of protection? If so, not true. The left just wants a degree of sanctuary, in that freedom of speech also means freedom from speech. To mandate certain speech is an infringement to the concept of 'freedom of speech.'"<P>I stated 'not so.' I then wrote 'mandate' as to what we should avoid. You claim I want the right to restrict the speech I don't want to hear. The truth is I don't want it shoved down my throat. Most importantly is that I don't want it shoved down my throat consistently at select venues, like government offices or State funded schools. I wouldn't want to prohibit someone’s right to lobby or publicly display their opinions or organizations intentions, so long as they have required permits. But what I object to is the continual presence of religious monikers affixed to public buildings, or religious chartings (prayer) occurring when I walk into public buildings that I help to support. You intentionally skewed the intentions of my statements. Because religion in America = Jesus. The left won't allow freedom of religion to be anything except Christianity, and that is restrictive. <P>"And yet, as a conservative (generally-the bipartisan comment was true), although I don't like having to hear certain speech I'm unwilling to restrict their freedom for the sake of mine."<P>What speech don't you like to hear? What if the rights of homosexuals were realized in schools or public buildings in regard to speech and decoration (decoration is a division of speech; ex: hanging symbols like the cross, etc), would you permit that? What if the rights of Satanists were recognized, hang the pentagram? I doubt it. The expression you stated about conservatives is just a chant that conservatives echo. When it comes down to brass-tacks, these items are narrowed to religious (Jesus specific)monikers.||||reg||1
