Z||000043||thomas_l||08-16-2001||03:31 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by EdsB52:<BR><B>It just seems improbable to find millionaires driving cars that are worth under $5,000.</B><P>Well Ed, you don't have to be poor to appreciate a fun car.<P><B>That’s great that one little push got you flying above the curve, but some people need to get kicked hard and never perform well, does that mean we cut off the assistance?</B> Yes <B> If so, where is the threshold where we give up?</B> When they fail or quit or don't pay.<B> If we refuse to help because someone isn't responding, is it an indication of that subject’s inaptitude or a statement of our (US citizens) uncompassionate nature?</B> The former. Nobody is owed a good life, nor guaranteed success. I never reached the point of being on the street or eating out of dumpsters, but the low points in my life were, without a doubt, achieved by my own bad decisions and poor priority setting.<B><P>"That is my main question with how much money is finally enough to be my fair share? " "This is a sincere question:  are you really saying that yes, you want other american's income to be GIVEN to you for work you haven't done?"<P>If we had a flat tax there would be no misunderstanding what we owe and how fair is fair. Most rich people don't want this because they have accountants and lawyers finding ways to relieve them from paying anything. Redistribution of wealth isn't compensation for work done, it's relief to people that need it. Yes, I want Americas wealth redistributed to other Americans who are in need.</B><BR>Flat tax? You contradict yourself. Doesn't the flat tax take food out of the mouths of poor people? Or does your version have exceptions? The current political system lets those in need define who is in need and how much wealth gets redistributed. You should be in favor of leaving things as-is.<P><B>What speech don't you like to hear? What if the rights of homosexuals were realized in schools or public buildings in regard to speech and decoration (decoration is a division of speech; ex: hanging symbols like the cross, etc), would you permit that? What if the rights of Satanists were recognized, hang the pentagram? I doubt it. The expression you stated about conservatives is just a chant that conservatives echo. When it comes down to brass-tacks, these items are narrowed to religious (Jesus specific)monikers.</B><P>Free speech is a matter of interpretation that relies on the "at-large" public's definition of acceptable. The problem is that a few agitators like to push that definition just for the benefit of public outrage and media hype. Then that whole discussion circles the toilet of "politically correct" and "protected classes". What outrages the nation may be of little concern to your neighbors and associates.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><p>[This message has been edited by thomas_l (edited 08-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000044||frontal lobe||08-16-2001||03:33 PM||||EdsB52, sorry I misconstrued the intent of the freedom of speech statements (genuine, not sarcasm).<P>I'm not against a society putting different religious icons, statements, etc. on public buildings if that is what that community wants, just because it isn't related to Jesus.  For example, heavily Jewish communities, star of David, whatever they use, fine.  Satanist stuff (like if I moved to some caribbean country, say, and that was their culture), if that's what the community wants, fine.  I've been to San Francisco several times--if that community wants to have a bunch of pro-homosexual statements on public buildings, I wouldn't like it but I wouldn't be willing to fight their freedom to express the prevalent sentiment of the community.  I can live with that without feeling like it's crammed down my throat.<P>Prayer in school--completely against it as a mandated group exercise.  Personal choice to do it when it doesn't interfere with the school activities (like teaching times, etc.)--all for it (individuals or voluntary groups).<P>Taxing to give to people in need-for it.  Key word-NEED.  Taxing to give for our current societies definition of "need"--mostly against it.  <P>For giving people opportunity, even to the inept.  Giving to people ongoing opportunities because of inaptitude because of their own laziness, lack of effort, or diligence-against.<P>I'm not a millionaire yet, but if I live another 10 years and nothing catastrophic happens, I will be.  But I don't care about that and one of the main reasons I own a Fiero and love hanging out here with you guys is because I don't think you guys care either.  We generally aren't trying to make a statement with our Fiero's because it isn't that kind of a car.  We just like them.  I like hanging out with people of all different walks of life, and ages, and interests, but I don't really get to do that in my work life.  Many interesting people at work, but a lot of common interests and viewpoints.  I like to learn things from you guys and your different perspectives, even guys like rayb.||||reg||1
Z||000045||EdsB52||08-16-2001||05:21 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by thomas_l:<BR><B>  [This message has been edited by thomas_l (edited 08-16-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Me-"It just seems improbable to find millionaires driving cars that are worth under $5,000."<BR>thomas 1-"Well Ed, you don't have to be poor to appreciate a fun car."<P>It's all about probability. It's aberrational to state there are millionaires driving Fieros.<P>Me-"That’s great that one little push got you flying above the curve, but some people need to get kicked hard and never perform well, does that mean we cut off the assistance? Yes If so, where is the threshold where we give up?"<BR>thomas 1-"When they fail or quit or don't pay. If we refuse to help because someone isn't responding, is it an indication of that subject’s inaptitude or a statement of our (US citizens) uncompassionate nature? The former. Nobody is owed a good life, nor guaranteed success. I never reached the point of being on the street or eating out of dumpsters, but the low points in my life were, without a doubt, achieved by my own bad decisions and poor priority setting."<P>Define failure; one missed attempt? So inaptitude is grounds for denial of assistance? How do retarded people or the physically handicapped fit into this? When they don't pay? How much time is allowed for someone to help himself or herself? You say no one is owed a good life or success. We do have Constitutional guarantees though, and some of those translate into financial aide. So actually yes, we are guaranteed elements of a good life. What if the poor decision-making is something genetically driven and the decisions aren't conscious? We already have the reputation for being a highly uncompassionate nation by the rest of the world. We've been removed from various associations due to that. So if we talk 'objective,' is the rest of the world a good enough sample size?<P>Me-"If we had a flat tax there would be no misunderstanding what we owe and how fair is fair. Most rich people don't want this because they have accountants and lawyers finding ways to relieve them from paying anything. Redistribution of wealth isn't compensation for work done, it's relief to people that need it. Yes, I want Americas wealth redistributed to other Americans who are in need."<BR>thomas 1-"Flat tax? You contradict yourself. Doesn't the flat tax take food out of the mouths of poor people? Or does your version have exceptions? The current political system lets those in need define who is in need and how much wealth gets redistributed. You should be in favor of leaving things as-is."<P>How do I contradict myself? GIVE EXAMPLES INSTEAD OF GRANDSTANDING (no insult intended, please reinforce your assertion).  How does the flat tax take food out of the mouths of poor people? The flat tax merely disallows the rich and corporations from loopholes. Every system has exceptions, such as earned income credit, but I would like to see a flat tax system with almost no loopholes. The current political system is a representational one. We vote in one jackass or another, that jackass does what he wants. You claim I should want to leave the system the way it is; truth is you're presenting your argument as mine and attempting to transfer it. I've argued my position and reinforced it, argue yours with your own rationale.<P>Me-"What speech don't you like to hear? What if the rights of homosexuals were realized in schools or public buildings in regard to speech and decoration (decoration is a division of speech; ex: hanging symbols like the cross, etc), would you permit that? What if the rights of Satanists were recognized, hang the pentagram? I doubt it. The expression you stated about conservatives is just a chant that conservatives echo. When it comes down to brass-tacks, these items are narrowed to religious (Jesus specific)monikers."<BR>thomas 1-"Free speech is a matter of interpretation that relies on the "at-large" public's definition of acceptable. The problem is that a few agitators like to push that definition just for the benefit of public outrage and media hype. Then that whole discussion circles the toilet of "politically correct" and "protected classes". What outrages the nation may be of little concern to your neighbors and associates."<P>What do you mean free speech interpretation is essentially defined by the public to be acceptable? Do you understand how the Constitution is interpreted? Does it really matter if the public accepts free speech? If so, to whom, you, me? Come on Thomas, we all know that free speech, like all other Constitutional Amendments are interpreted by all appellate courts, especially the US Supreme Court. Very, very, very indirectly do we get a say in the matter. US Supreme Court Justices are appointed by the President and confirmed by the senate. We get to vote for these President and senators, but by the time these politicians get around to appointing a Justice, things are so diluted that we really don't have a say in that. For instance, most Justices are on the bench for sometimes decades, so our positions may have changed twice while that Justice is still there, and that accounts for us being for him or her being appointed. Then say a few agitators push the envelope. Are you referring to amicus curiae? There are amicus on both sides of issues submitting briefs. Are you calling the other side agitators? Don't you think the other side calls your side agitators? Media, let's bring in one corrupt organization at a time without convoluting them. You think classes should not be protected? Like whom, the physically handicapped, homosexual, midgets, women, minorities, the aged, or whom?<P>Now, Thomas, I've answered all of your questions as thoroughly as is reasonable without being too much of a bandwidth thief. If not, tell me where to expound. So when you reply, answer all asked questions plus the ones you missed in the first posting please.<BR>1) Separately define handicapped and disadvantaged people.<BR>2) How many chances do disadvantaged people get?<BR>3) How many chances do handicapped people get?<BR>4) What do we do with them once their chances run out?<BR>5) How will that disposition affect the rest of society?<BR>6) Do you understand how we do have Constitutional guarantees that mandate certain luxuries in this country?<BR>7) Explain the flat tax contradiction.<BR>8) Where does the 'at-large' vote play in to the construction of free speech laws?<BR>9) Expound on your free speech parameters; do you feel the cross should be hung in schools and/or courtrooms? Is there room for other religions? If so, should the pentagram be hung next to the cross if people petition for it? The homosexual rate in America is approximately 10%. Should they have rights to free speech in they way they want? If so, without limit?||||reg||1
Z||000046||JSocha||08-16-2001||05:31 PM||||Does anybody else concur?:   <A HREF="http://www.twinoaks4fun.com/sounds/enufcrap.wav" TARGET=_blank>Hear it be spoken</A>||||reg||1
Z||000047||Galen||08-16-2001||06:36 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by EdsB52:<BR><B> Aren't we supposed to be touchy-feely here? Why start this?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I got nothing against ya, I was just point out you talk alot  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> But, if you try to get all touchy and feely on me, expect to have a few bodyparts removed. <P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="purple">Ray</FONT> <FONT COLOR="gray">-</FONT> <FONT COLOR="purple">Fierolinks v1.0 Coming Very Soon</FONT><BR>In the event I offended you, Let me state this apology. - I am incredibly sorry for my personal views, which I am granted under united states constitution to have, offended you in anyway. If you do not accept this apology. Please by all means, tell me! -||||reg||1
Z||000048||thomas_l||08-16-2001||07:57 PM||||Ed - I'm not grandstanding. You made some insightful points and I offered my opinions. Nothing was in the form of a question in your assertions or my responses. I'll expand on my opinions briefly:<P><I>Government financial aid</I> - I said, "Nobody is owed a good life, nor guaranteed success." The US Constitution affirms some basic human rights but does not specify income redistribution. You and many others feel that this is implied. I disagree. Note: I did not say that nobody should get government aid. Nor did I say that all protected classes should not have protection. My opinion is that these benefits are heavily abused and out of control. Mainly due to the conflict of interest noted in the next section on taxes.<P><I>Flat tax</I> - This one's easy. A flat percentage applied across the board puts the poor at a disadvantage. For example, if the tax was 15% a family just getting by on $10k per year would lose $1.5k to income tax - cutting deep into the bare minimum it takes to survive. The family on $100k would lose $15k to taxes leaving them still pretty fat on meeting minimums. Read what I wrote carefully: <I>"The current political system lets those in need define who is in need and how much wealth gets redistributed."</I> I wasn't alluding to the outcome of the presidential election. I was pointing out what I feel is a flaw in our current system that you <I>seem</I> (my apology if this is inaccurate) to agree with. That being the fact that the number of people that solely depend on government aid in many cases is becoming the majority vote in deciding that very policy, or more accurately the political figure that supports their desired policies. It's a conflict of interest in my opinion. Soon the "tax<I>payer</I>" will be in the minority and there will be no limits.<P><I>Free speech</I> - I said,"Free speech is a matter of interpretation that relies on the "at-large" public's definition of acceptable." If people display a portrait of Jesus in a Christian community, there wouldn't be much said. If the same portrait were displayed in an Islamic community, there would be outrage. My point was that there are agitators that would do the latter just for the sake of doing it, claiming that the community's outrage is racist and discriminatory, for no good reason other than to get their mug on the news.<P>OK - I'm through. Respond if you like, but the rest of the audience here might want the long posts to go off into e-mail land. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000049||Mach10||08-16-2001||09:26 PM||||I'm about as liberal as they come. *sigh* All right, one at a time. Assuming these are quotes ASSOCIATED with the liberal party(ies) I offer these thoughts for your consideration, dear reader:<P><B>1) I must sacrifice some degree of personal liberty to be safe. (Or, I will be safer without a gun)</B><BR>I agree totally. If I NEED a gun to be safe, then there is a problem with society. It's not my job to maintain law and order. That responsibility falls on the Police and Civil guard. Owning guns isn't a bad thing, but if you consider it your only line of defence, something is SERIOUSLY wrong.<P><B>2)Keeping more of my money makes me greedy, and hurts the economy. In fact, it is my civic duty to have 40% of my income confiscated.</B><BR>That statement makes no sense. The only way to truly remove your cash from the economy is to bury it in the garden (There is good money in Tomatoes). Your money STILL does work when sitting in a bank account. <P><B>3)SUV's are the worst invention of all time, are driven by evil, cigar chomping capitalists, and will eventually cause an environmental meltdown, which will end the world.</B><BR>Yes, but only because they are ugly and promote a driving attitude that that is counter-productive. SUVs are more like rolex watches in this respect. Lots of money for a shiny object. A casio will tell time almost as well.<P><BR><B>4)The so-called news media is the 4th branch of government.</B><BR>Both wings say that. Why pick on liberals? The media is commonly associated with the fungus that feeds on pond-scum. Leeches feeding on human misery and scandal. the only SAD part is that the public laps it up.<P><B>5)All speech is protected by the United States Constitution, unless it's "hate speech" (i.e., religious or politically conservative).</B><BR>I am canadian, so it only SORT OF applies. As far as our own freedom of speech goes:<BR>Protect your right to say what you want. However, you can't excercise one right while violating anothers. Religious doesn't mean Hate. Neither does Conservative. Then again, some pretty awful materials have been released by both. Of course, you could say that "Freedom of Speech" is the priority, and have "Uncle Grand Dragon's KKK-funhouse" aired beside Teletubbies.<P><BR>ACK! Have to stop. I will continue this later...||||reg||12
Z||000050||DaRkLoRD||08-16-2001||10:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by frontal lobe:<BR><B>Prayer in school--completely against it as a mandated group exercise.  Personal choice to do it when it doesn't interfere with the school activities (like teaching times, etc.)--all for it (individuals or voluntary groups).<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I got in trouble many times in high school for refusing to listen to the lords prayer each morning.. I was usually late, so I just wanted to quietly walk to class. If I wanted to be taught religion, I'd go to church. but I went to school to learn other stuff.. so after a few times of being called out of class for refusing to stop, stand patiently in the hall and show respect while the prayer was recited over the PA system.... I complained to the principal. she told me that there wasn't enough demand for other religions in the school.. I just wanted to be excused from the MANDATORY religious practices each morning.. keep in mind this was a public high school.. I think people should be able to practice whatever religion they choose.. but I don't want to be punished for not participating if I don't want to. <P>I don't think that's out of line.<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000051||DaRkLoRD||08-16-2001||10:21 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<BR><B><BR>rastiafarians<BR>rainbow children<BR>native american church<BR>wickica<BR>muratisum<BR>zorasterisum<BR>zen<BR>shintoisum<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>and FYI (this is not a flame..) ray b, it's spelled "wicca".. that's where the word "witch" came from, though if anyone calls my sister a witch, or asks if she has green skin and warts.... you'll likely get kicked in the groin.. though she does dress in black most of the time.. hehe.. (which means nothing, I wear black clothes most of the time also, but I'm not wiccan.. though if I did want to join an organized religion, wicca would be in my top 5 list.)<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000052||EdsB52||08-16-2001||10:31 PM||||Hey Thomas, I hope you didn't take that wrong! I was just alluding to you accusing me of contradicting. My personal feelings are that if you impose a general opinion on someone you should reinforce it with proof. If you cast a tangible accusation at someone, you need to back it with quotes within context. In this forum it's easy to retrieve references to reinforce or refute someone's statements. I have to go to my girlfriends now, but I'll answer these good-looking post later, Ed.||||reg||1
Z||000053||frontal lobe||08-16-2001||11:40 PM||||DaRkLoRD,<P>I just wanted to be sure you know we are agreeing here, right?  The personal choice remark was if the STUDENTS made a personal choice to do it, not the teachers or staff.||||reg||1
Z||000054||Cheever3000||08-16-2001||11:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by frontal lobe:<BR>Prayer in school--completely against it as a mandated group exercise.  Personal choice to do it when it doesn't interfere with the school activities (like teaching times, etc.)--all for it (individuals or voluntary groups).<HR></BLOCKQUOTE>Yeah, me too.  And I know my private Christian school-teacher wife agrees as well.  Of course, we're taking about public school, like DaRkLoRD said.<P><BR>||||reg||1
