Z||000060||EdsB52||08-18-2001||06:29 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<BR><B>poor fools nobody who drives a fiero is rich enuff to get in the republican's inner club.why workers would want the masters to get richer,at their expence is beyond me.<BR>btw the real tax on long term $$$ is 20% and falling now but workers still pay for near 15% ss tax plus income tax and that adds up to way more than the rich pay now.<BR>bush and his gang stold the election and now want to steal the surpluss instead of paying the past do debts.<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This is a perfect statement. I think the answer resides in the notion that total subservience to the ideologies of rich people makes the peasants feel protected by the rich. I think a school of though exists where the peasants feel the rich will take them into their club if they subscribe to and protect those ideologies. Maybe the bailiff or cop mentality. They put their ass on the line for pennies to protect the will of the rich and powerful, thinking they're also in the club, when in reality they're dogs just like every other unimportant citizen in the eyes of the rich. ||||reg||1
Z||000061||EdsB52||08-18-2001||06:38 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroLisa:<BR><B>ray, you make me laugh. "nobody who drives a fiero is rich enuff to get in the republican's inner club" <P>I won't sit here and preach about how much money I have, or that you have, but some people have Fieros because they like to buy things that are fun and fairly practical. Just because I could afford a Porsche doesn't mean I don't have other things to spend my money on.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ray isn't talking absolutes or aberrations, he's merely saying that most millionaires are not going to drive Fieros. Why? Because they are junk! To us, they're fun little cars that take up lots of time and are unique. But they're a nuisance that would be best suited in the junkyard to the rich. When Ray talks about the rich, he's not defining the casual millionaire with new money that just won the lottery or got an inheritance, he's talking about the established rich that also have influence. If I win the powerball for 120M, I can say that I would still have a Fiero in my collection, probably not an everyday daily driver though.||||reg||1
Z||000062||EdsB52||08-18-2001||06:50 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Galen:<BR><B>So I guess you dont like get 'stem'ulated by stem cell research? Anyone remember.. "The good of the many outweigh the good of the few or the one"? sacrificing one 'unborn human' is not worth helping other born humans? <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>"The good of the many outweigh the good of the few or the one"<P>You are referring to utilitarianism with that quote. Who originally wrote/said it? I'm not sure to which angle you heading with this. Is this sarcasm? By the way you classify an embryo as an unborn human, I would say you disagree with Stem Cell Research. You do realize the US Supreme Court doesn't recognize a human embryo, or for that matter a fertilized egg or even a first trimester fetus as a human, right? In many cases, a second trimester fetus doesn't have full recognition of fetus status. So now we'll all curse the Supreme Court and pronounce their lack of right to cast decisions such as these (at least those of you that disagree with them in this area), but then you applaud them when they make a decision that suits or liking, such as prayer in school.<P><BR>So if indeed you disagree with the killing of humans, and you disagree with utilitarianism, then you're stuck in a contradiction, the death penalty.||||reg||1
Z||000063||EdsB52||08-18-2001||06:55 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Patrick's Dad:<BR><B>&lt;quote&gt;So I guess you dont like get 'stem'ulated by stem cell research? Anyone remember..<BR>                       "The good of the many outweigh(s) the good of the few or the one"? sacrificing one 'unborn human' is not worth helping other born humans?&lt;/quote&gt;<P>Galen, Mr. Spock &lt;i&gt;chose&lt;/i&gt; to give his life for the many, and his Katra was in Dr. McCoy anyway.<P>Why don't we just take a random human being, with out their consent, and tear their limbs off (D&C abortion), or burn them to death (saline), or poke a hole in the back of their necks and suck out the contents of their skull (PBA). After all, more food, more air, and more money for the rest of us....<P>This ought to get me a stern warning from Cliff....<P>[This message has been edited by Patrick's Dad (edited 08-15-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>So you're concerned with the rights of the few? And you're also concerned with the rights of humans. How do you feel about mass imprisonment and the death penalty? You can't be concerned with the first two and also be for the third, unless you're like most religious based, right-win conservatives.||||reg||1
Z||000064||EdsB52||08-18-2001||07:01 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Galen:<BR><B> I got nothing against ya, I was just point out you talk alot   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> But, if you try to get all touchy and feely on me, expect to have a few bodyparts removed. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>See, and if I respond the way I want to, we'll have WWIII again. Kidding or not, keep your idle threats to yourself or you will be the cause of the next blowup.||||reg||1
Z||000065||EdsB52||08-18-2001||07:13 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cheever3000:<BR><B> [QUOTE]Originally posted by ray b:<BR>$$$$$ for bible schools<HR></BLOCKQUOTE></B>[/QUOTE]<P>"private Christian schools <B>don't <I>want</I></B> government money, because there are strings attached."<P>What strings? Bush would place much higher contingencies on 'liberal money' than conservative money.<P>"For your information, they don't exist because someone says "I think I'll start a school and push my religion on everybody".  They exist because grassroots Americans <B><I>support</I></B> them.  Not just by sending their kids there, but by volunteering their own time, sweat, talents and abilities to help out."<P>And you're right. I believe the original intent of private Christian schools is genuine. The problem is the byproduct that results from Christian schools. I feel they impose a belief system onto young minds that is not objective. Just as I disagree with homosexual parents having the right to adopt children (sorry to everyone that has me pegged for a flaming liberal, I'm not), I disagree with anything else coercive being introduced into a child’s life.||||reg||1
Z||000066||EdsB52||08-18-2001||07:24 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by frontal lobe:<BR>[B]rayb, [B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>"if there is a school choice program where the parents get the money "from the government", and then the parents choose where the children go, I don't care if the parents decide to send them to a school such as you listed.  That is NOT government supporting certain schools.  The PARENTS have the FREEDOM to choose."<P>Ya you're right there FL, but the criticism lies in that the poor won't use this money to subsidize their children’s private school tuition (I don't believe it is designed to pay for all just part), as they can't afford the rest of the tuition and costs. The criticism is that this will be used to subsidize rich people that would send their kids to private schools government assistance or not. Allegedly, just another way to redistribute wealth. I'm not disagreeing with you, just stating one popular critique.<P>"And I love this, "We can't take government money to give to support private schools".  The government got the money from private citizens.  So why it can't go back to private citizens for THEM to decide how to spend it is just beyond me."<P>I don't think anyone will successfully argue whether the government needs to 'give the money back to the people.' What people do argue is the distribution of that money. Ever drive in West L.A.? How about Beverly Hills? If so, what did you think of the roads and availability of police and other services? It's all about distribution; not the if, but the where.||||reg||1
Z||000067||EdsB52||08-18-2001||07:50 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by frontal lobe:<BR>[B][B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>"I'm not against a society putting different religious icons, statements, etc. on public buildings if that is what that community wants, just because it isn't related to Jesus. "<P>Well then forget praising Jesus, praise you! That's a great objective attitude to have, just not shared by many in religious circles. They (Christians) feel they have sole license do dispense only Christianity, and that no other religion is 'strong' enough to count. The First Amendment reads, "Congress shall make no law respecting an establishment of religion, or prohibit the free exercise thereof." That's just an excerpt, but the problem there is that the President or Supreme Court apparently can do the preceding.<P>"For example, heavily Jewish communities, star of David, whatever they use, fine.  Satanist stuff (like if I moved to some caribbean country, say, and that was their culture), if that's what the community wants, fine.  I've been to San Francisco several times--if that community wants to have a bunch of pro-homosexual statements on public buildings, I wouldn't like it but I wouldn't be willing to fight their freedom to express the prevalent sentiment of the community.  I can live with that without feeling like it's crammed down my throat."<P>Ya, but does equal protection also mean equal representation? I mean this as strict question, not rhetorical. You can't dictate what a certain region or city should like or subscribe to, but I think the intent of equal protection means somewhat, 'vanilla ice cream.' <P>"Prayer in school--completely against it as a mandated group exercise.  Personal choice to do it when it doesn't interfere with the school activities (like teaching times, etc.)--all for it (individuals or voluntary groups)."<P>What if they allowed it and it offended passers by? What if a student couldn't avoid it by sheer audible volume of the prayers? What if a group of kids were praying in a satanic fashion over the pentagram? I think the Supreme Court was right years ago when they abolished prayer from churches altogether.<P>"For giving people opportunity, even to the inept.  Giving to people ongoing opportunities because of inaptitude because of their own laziness, lack of effort, or diligence-against."<P>Lazy is subjective. Who defines guidelines? Who defines mental handicap from laziness? Might some handicapped people be left out if we draw a hard line? Are we utilitarian or not?||||reg||1
Z||000068||Slapnutz2000||08-18-2001||12:20 PM||||<I>1) I must sacrifice some degree of personal liberty to be safe. (Or, I will be safer without a gun)</I><P>I don't think guns should be taken away but the Constitution gives Americans the right to bear arms IN ORDER TO FORM A MILITIA.  You think your rifles and pistols could stop the Federal military from taking whatever they want whenever they want?  Of course not.<P><I>2)Keeping more of my money makes me greedy, and hurts the economy. In fact, it is my civic duty to have 40% of my income confiscated.</I><P>Republicans have always given larger tax breaks to the richest 1% and to to large corporations.  The voting record shows this statement as fact.<P><I>3)SUV's are the worst invention of all time, are driven by evil, cigar chomping capitalists, and will eventually cause an environmental meltdown, which will end the world.</I><P>Actually, soccer moms drive most SUVs.  Global warming is a concern.  Albeit, I'm FAR from a tree hugger.<P><I>4)The so-called news media is the 4th branch of government.</I><P>From what I've seen, there is a large CONSERVATIVE bias in the media.  Pres. Bush is a stupid man and his "faith based initiatives" will only go to help Christian organizations which isn't fair to jews and muslims.  I'm an athiest and religion is a complete joke to me.<P><I>5)All speech is protected by the United States Constitution, unless it's "hate speech" (i.e., religious or politically conservative).</I><P>The only speech that should not be protected is slander and libel.  Anything else has the right to be heard.<P><I>6)Morality is not black and white, but is defined by individual choice. (Or, feelings are mightier than truth)</I><P>My morals are different from most people's.  I don't base my life off the words of someone who lived 2000 years ago because they do not mean anything to me today.<P><I>7)All life is precious, especially that of suckerfish, spotted owls, and various unnamed foliage in the rain forests. Unborn human babies, however, are unviable tissue masses, and therefore unworthy of protection under the law (unless they are harvested for medical research).</I><P>I agree with the animal bit but I don't see anything wrong in trashing an embryo or fetus that isn't wanted to is deformed.  It's called population control.<P><I>8)The United States has the worst healthcare system in the entire known universe, and must instead revert to a socialized system which has failed miserably everywhere else it's been tried.</I><P>Canada seems to be doing pretty well with their state-run system.  The US model is far from perfect but it isn't altogether failing by any means.<P><I>9)The Federal Government is the provider, protector, and empowerer of all people.</I><P>The conservative viewpoint is: "MY CHURCH is the protector and empowerer of all people." <P><I>10)Rich people are vermin, and acquired their wealth on the backs of the poor. For this, they should be severely punished by litigation and taxation.</I><P>Nah.  They made their wealth mostly from either inheritance (old money) or off the work of other people who aren't necessarily poor.<P>BTW, I'm a registered Libertarian and I'm FAR from a liberal...||||reg||1
